# The smoke subset.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
#
# WHAT THIS IS. The smallest set of Go tests that would catch a broken build,
# runnable in well under a minute. It is the check you run before you walk away
# from a change, not the check that lets you ship it. The full gate is
# scripts/gate.sh and it is the only thing that gates anything.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO, stated here so a green smoke run cannot be
# mistaken for a green gate:
#
#   - no race detector. The race detector is what turns internal/xcfg from
#     roughly 15 seconds into 327 (measured 2026-08-30, on this machine, in the
#     two runs recorded in that day's gate log and in a plain run of the same
#     package). A smoke subset that carries it is not a smoke subset.
#   - no coverage floors.
#   - one or two tests per package, not the package.
#
# So: smoke green and gate red is an ordinary outcome, not a contradiction.
#
# HOW TO RUN IT
#
#     bash scripts/smoke.sh
#
# FORMAT
#
#   <package path from the module root>  <regex passed to go test -run>
#
# One package per line. Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A BUILD TAG. A //go:build smoke tag would need a separate
# file per package and would put the membership decision in the file being
# tested, where it is invisible from anywhere else. This list is one place,
# readable without grepping, and test/smoke/registry_test.go checks every entry
# against the source: a test named here that no longer exists is a failure, not
# a silently empty -run pattern. That last part is the whole point. "go test
# -run TestThatWasRenamed" exits 0 having executed nothing, which is the exact
# shape of a green run that tested no code.

# Compiles, parses and validates a real share link end to end, which is the
# fastest thing in the repository that would notice a broken parser.
internal/link          ^TestEmittedConfigPassesEngineValidate$

# The engine's own lifecycle. If this fails the appliance cannot start at all.
internal/engine        ^TestStartAndStop$|^TestStopBeforeStartIsNotAnError$

# The state file round trip, both directions, plus the redaction rule.
internal/state         ^TestRoundTripEveryField$|^TestNothingRendersASecret$

# The two generated configuration files, and the committed engine documents
# put back through the engine's own loader.
internal/hotspot       ^TestRenderHostapdGolden$|^TestRenderDnsmasqGolden$|^TestGoldenVariants_Hostapd$
internal/xcfg          ^TestGoldenFilesAreAcceptedByTheEngine$

# The firewall the box installs.
internal/netcfg        ^TestGolden_RulesetCaptured$

# The panel: the polled document and the route table. TestGolden_RenderedPages
# is deliberately NOT here; it renders eighteen pages and belongs in the gate.
internal/panel         ^TestGolden_StatusJSON$|^TestGolden_RouteInventory$

# The privileged service's start ordering, which is where a fail-open would
# live if one existed.
internal/privsvc       ^TestStartAppliesThingsInTheRequiredOrder$|^TestAFailedStartLeavesNothingApplied$

# Real traffic through a real xray-core server: one row per protocol the parser
# accepts, driven from a pasted share link. The passing direction is well under a
# second. The mutation half, TestEveryCarriageProofCanFail, runs the whole table
# again once per injected defect and is deliberately left to the gate.
test/tunnel            ^TestEachProtocolCarriesRealTrafficThroughARealServer$|^TestTheProofRejectsARequestThatDidNotGoThroughTheTunnel$

# The leak scan over every committed fixture, including the planted-secret
# proof that the scanner can still fail.
test/goldenscan        ^TestNoCommittedFixtureCarriesACredential$|^TestScannerCatchesAPlantedSecretOfEveryClass$

# The command line: it must still build and its check subcommand must still run
# without touching the box.
cmd/caspian            ^TestCheckChangesNothingAndSaysWhatItLookedAt$
